    <!-- EDITORIAL & METHODOLOGICAL KNOWLEDGE SECTION (E-E-A-T) -->
    <article style="border-top: 1px solid #e2e8f0; padding-top: 3.5rem; line-height: 1.85; color: #334155;">
      
      <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3.5vw, 1.85rem); font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-bottom: 1rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; line-height: 1.3;">
        Theoretical Foundations: Logarithmic Ion Scales &amp; Exact Quadratic Equilibrium
      </h2>
      <p>
        In aqueous analytical chemistry, the concentrations of hydronium ions and hydroxide ions span more than 14 orders of magnitude. The logarithmic pH scale was developed to compress this exponential dynamic range into an accessible linear index:
      </p>

      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #0284c7; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; overflow-x: auto;">
        pH = -log10[H+] &lt;==&gt; [H+] = 10^(-pH)
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        1. Autoionization of Water &amp; Temperature-Dependent K_w
      </h3>
      <p>
        Pure water undergoes endothermic self-ionization, characterized by the ion product constant K_w = [H+][OH-]. Because the dissociation is endothermic, increasing the temperature drives the forward equilibrium, increasing K_w and reducing pK_w:
      </p>

      <div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <table style="width: 100%; min-width: 580px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.88rem; text-align: left;">
          <thead style="background: #f8fafc; border-bottom: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1;">
            <tr>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; color: #0f172a; font-weight: 700;">Temperature (&deg;C)</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; color: #0f172a; font-weight: 700;">Ion Product (K_w)</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; color: #0f172a; font-weight: 700;">pK_w</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; color: #0f172a; font-weight: 700;">Neutral pH (1/2 pK_w)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #f1f5f9;">
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-weight: bold; color: #0284c7;">0 &deg;C (Freezing)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">1.14 &times; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8309;</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">14.94</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace; font-weight: bold;">7.47</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #f1f5f9; background: #fafafa;">
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-weight: bold; color: #0284c7;">25 &deg;C (IUPAC Standard)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">1.00 &times; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8308;</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">14.00</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace; font-weight: bold;">7.00</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #f1f5f9;">
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-weight: bold; color: #0284c7;">37 &deg;C (Human Physiological)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">2.42 &times; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8308;</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">13.62</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace; font-weight: bold;">6.81</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #f1f5f9; background: #fafafa;">
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-weight: bold; color: #0284c7;">60 &deg;C (Elevated Thermal)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">9.61 &times; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8308;</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">13.02</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace; font-weight: bold;">6.51</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        2. Weak Acid Dissociation &amp; The Exact Quadratic Formula
      </h3>
      <p>
        For a weak monoprotic acid, setting up the ICE (Initial, Change, Equilibrium) table yields an exact quadratic relationship:
      </p>

      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #10b981; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; overflow-x: auto;">
        K_a = x^2 / (C0 - x) ==&gt; x^2 + K_a * x - K_a * C0 = 0
      </div>
      <p>
        While textbooks often recommend approximating x as the square root of Ka times C0, this shortcut is only valid when the degree of dissociation is less than or equal to 5%. Above 5%, the exact analytical quadratic root is required:
      </p>

      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; overflow-x: auto;">
        x = [H+] = ( -K_a + sqrt(K_a^2 + 4 * K_a * C0) ) / 2
      </div>

      <!-- Worked Step-by-Step Problem -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        3. Worked Benchmark Example: Dilute Formic Acid (5% Rule Failure Case)
      </h3>
      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Problem Statement:</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569;">
          Calculate the pH and degree of ionization of a dilute 0.0010 M solution of formic acid (HCOOH, K_a = 1.77 &times; 10&#8315;&#8308;) at 25 &deg;C.
        </p>

        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 1: Simplified Approximation Shortcut</p>
        <div style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569; font-family: monospace; line-height: 1.6;">
          <span>x_approx = sqrt(K_a * C0) = sqrt(1.77e-4 * 1.0e-3) = 4.207 &times; 10⁻⁴ M</span><br />
          <span>alpha_approx = (4.207e-4 / 0.0010) &times; 100% = <strong>42.07%</strong></span><br />
          <span>Verdict: FAILS (42.07% &gt; 5%). Introduces a 23.3% relative error.</span>
        </div>

        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 2: Exact Analytical Quadratic Root</p>
        <div style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569; font-family: monospace; line-height: 1.6;">
          <span>x^2 + (1.77e-4)x - (1.77e-7) = 0</span><br />
          <span>x = [ -1.77e-4 + sqrt((1.77e-4)^2 + 4 * 1.77e-7) ] / 2</span><br />
          <span>x = [ -1.77e-4 + sqrt(3.133e-8 + 7.08e-7) ] / 2 = <strong>3.411 &times; 10⁻⁴ M</strong></span><br />
          <span>pH = -log10(3.411 &times; 10⁻⁴) = <strong>3.467</strong></span><br />
          <span>Exact alpha = (3.411 &times; 10⁻⁴ / 0.0010) &times; 100% = <strong>34.11%</strong></span>
        </div>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #059669; font-weight: 700;">
          Analytical Takeaway: Exact quadratic solving guarantees laboratory and academic accuracy for dilute solutions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- NIST / IUPAC ACID-BASE DISSOCIATION BENCHMARK TABLE -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        4. NIST &amp; IUPAC Aqueous Dissociation &amp; Indicator Standards (25 &deg;C)
      </h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #475569; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        Standard dissociation constants (K_a, pK_a, K_b) and visual indicator pH transition intervals:
      </p>

      <div style="border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <table style="width: 100%; min-width: 650px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.85rem; text-align: left; background: #ffffff;">
          <thead style="background: #0f172a; color: white;">
            <tr>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid #334155;">Electrolyte / Indicator</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid #334155;">Chemical Formula</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid #334155;">Dissociation Type</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid #334155;">K Constant (25 &deg;C)</th>
              <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; border: 1px solid #334155;">pK Value / Transition Range</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0;">
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700;">Hydrochloric Acid</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">HCl</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;">Strong Monoprotic Acid</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">&gt;&gt; 1 (Complete)</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; color: #475569;">pKa &approx; -6.3</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0; background: #fafafa;">
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-weight: 700;">Formic Acid</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">HCOOH</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;">Weak Carboxylic Acid</td>
              <td style="padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;">Ka = 1.77 &times; 10&#8315;&#8308;</td>
