      </div>

      <!-- FAQ Accordion -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: pH, pOH &amp; Aqueous Equilibrium
      </h3>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why is neutral pH not always exactly 7.00?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Neutral pH is strictly defined where [H&#8314;] = [OH&#8315;], which corresponds to half of pK_w. Because the autoionization of water is endothermic, K_w increases with temperature. At 25 &deg;C, K_w = 1.00 &times; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8308; (neutral pH = 7.00), but at 37 &deg;C, K_w = 2.42 &times; 10&#8315;&#185;&#8308;, which makes pure neutral water pH 6.81.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How does a digital calculator simulate universal indicator paper and universal indicator solution color scales?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            While physical universal indicator paper and universal indicator solution rely on dye mixtures that change protonation states across laboratory samples, the digital calculator computes the exact hydronium concentration [H+] and maps it to a calibrated RGB visual spectrum replicating 1-14 color bands.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">When does the 5% approximation rule for weak acid calculations fail?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            The 5% approximation shortcut assumes that x is much smaller than C0. This fails whenever the degree of dissociation exceeds 5%, which happens with relatively strong weak acids or at low analytical concentrations below 0.0001 M. In these cases, exact quadratic formulas must be used.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How do universal indicator paper ph 1 14 strips compare to precise logarithmic calculations?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Physical universal indicator paper ph 1 14 strips provide rapid semi-quantitative color estimates with an uncertainty of roughly +/- 0.5 pH units. In contrast, digital logarithmic calculations and quadratic equilibrium engines yield precise values to three decimal places.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Can pH ever be negative or greater than 14?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Yes. The standard 0 to 14 scale is merely a practical reference range for dilute aqueous solutions. Concentrated hydrochloric acid (12 M HCl) has a theoretical pH of approximately -1.08, and saturated sodium hydroxide (19 M NaOH) yields a pH above 15.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the mathematical relationship linking Ka, Kb, and Kw?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            For any conjugate acid-base pair in aqueous solution, the product of Ka and Kb equals the autoionization constant of water: K_a &times; K_b = K_w (or pKa + pKb = pKw = 14.00 at 25 &deg;C).
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

    </article>

